Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Anonymous
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 3/21/2025
Notes: Period live-action drama; 1990s Milwaukee into later Seattle; protagonist on-screen cue is NARRATOR until the book names him at the close

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A tall brick fifth-floor walk-up. Traffic hums. A burnt-orange 1980 Trans Am with yellow flames idles at the curb, eagle on the hood.

(SOUND: city traffic, distant bus air-brakes)

NARRATOR V.O.
Big brother was mine. Not the stupid book. My real Big Brother.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Cramped, lived-in. MA, sixties, tired brown eyes, brown hair, cardigan, sits half-dozing in a worn chair before a daytime game show. NARRATOR, late twenties, lean, unremarkable clothes, pizza-work shoes, carries grocery bags in.

(SOUND: game-show applause from the TV, floorboards creak)

NICK shoulders in behind him—thirties, reddish-brown hair in a mess, scar over left eyebrow, scarred chin, steel-worker build, boots, suede jacket—six bags hanging off one arm like a strongman act.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that.

NARRATOR
I know.

NICK
That's how old people go downhill. Pretty soon they don't want to go outside or eat or nothing.

Ma stirs, vague smile. Nick kisses her cheek, drops keys in a basket by the door.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. Face like a puzzle held with glue-gun stuff. A modern-day Viking.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Sun on brick. Kids' kickball. Dented garbage-can lids for bases. YOUNG NARRATOR, about eight, watches. YOUNG NICK, twelve, already big, hair wild.

(SOUND: rubber ball thud, kids yelling, a window smash)

The ball rockets through OLD MRS. ENGEL's kitchen glass. LITTLE MARK freezes, terrified. Young Nick steps up to the furious neighbor.

YOUNG NICK
I did it.

NARRATOR V.O.
Ma had to pay for the window. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks. He never told her it was Mark.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Nick pushes through the door. Inside, a man mid-preacher-curl with a forty-pound bar sees him, drops the weight, offers the bench. Nick nods, racks a fifty for warm-ups.

(SOUND: iron plates clank, gym echo)

NARRATOR V.O.
When he walked in the bar or the gym, people moved. He goes where he wants. Nobody questions that guy.

EXT. METAL FACTORY - DAY

Nick in hard hat at a crane cab, then on the line separating big metal pieces. Later he peels out in the Trans Am, T-top flashing.

(SOUND: factory horn, V8 rumble)

NARRATOR V.O.
He runs the assembly line. Easy money. Nice car. Cool place on Werner Street. Five blocks from Ma and me.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Steam, flour dust. NARRATOR spins dough under heat lamps. TONY, older owner, watches from the counter. Outside the front glass: Milwaukee sidewalk.

(SOUND: oven doors slam, order ticket printer)

NARRATOR V.O.
Somebody has to be here with Ma. She's getting older. Hard for her to see. I make ends meet on pizza money. Nick says you don't need a degree. Just know somebody.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

NARRATOR locks his bike. A skinny young woman with short brown hair, pale skin, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, blue tennis shoes, backpack—SIONNA—trips on the cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: shoe scuff on concrete, street noise)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. Their eyes lock—hers a watery early-morning blue.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon. If you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes a number, walks away, glances back over her shoulder. He stands rooted.

(SOUND: her footsteps fade on pavement)

NARRATOR V.O.
No girl ever interested in me. It was like I knew her. Intense. Like tides coming in—and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Pill bottles lined on the counter. Ma paces, glassy-eyed, mumbling, hunting.

(SOUND: bottles rattle, fridge hum)

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Mrs. Canasta lives down one level. Your pills are right in front of you.

She stares as if she doesn't know him, then snaps back.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don't know what got into me.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on the TV. Smoke, pool, darts. NARRATOR at the bar. Nick slaps his back, Jack and Coke in hand.

(SOUND: crowd roar from TV, billiard crack, rock under the talk)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

NARRATOR
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up. Why don't you take her to the store?
Holy Christ, do something before we pay for a nursing home.
They take every last dime. Is that what you want?

NARRATOR
Of course not. I just needed to get away a while.

Nick's look could punch. Narrator pays, leaves into cold air.

EXT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Narrator hurries under old lights. Enters.

(SOUND: wind, then heavy door thud, quiet hush inside)

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Wood floors, chain-hung lamps, classic literature stacks. LIBRARIAN at desk. Narrator fills a form, gets a temporary paper card, pulls Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: stamp on card, pages riffling)

NARRATOR V.O.
Who needed school when everything is free in this building?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. Narrator reads under a lamp—Heathcliff, Catherine. Sleep takes him.

(SOUND: distant traffic, page turn, then dream wash)

EXT. ENGLISH MOOR - DAY (DREAM)

Wind on heather. Sionna's face in old clothes, riding, laughing through outdoors.

(SOUND: wind howl, hoofbeats)

NARRATOR V.O.
Sionna's face. Old clothes. I got this feeling I would end up feeling for her just like Heathcliff.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - BEDROOM - NIGHT

He jolts half-awake, book on his chest, stares at the ceiling.

(SOUND: his sharp breath, radiator knock)

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, notebooks. Sionna at a back wall table in loose green hooded top. Narrator sits. Those light blue eyes.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low indie music, murmur)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
Yea, I know. I read it.

She talks with her hands—ghosts, double characters, wild moors, infertile ground.

SIONNA
The love Heathcliff has never changes. In the end that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR V.O.
It reminded me of Nick. Strong and unwavering. I wondered if it would destroy him too.

SIONNA
Friday night. Dinner.

He nods, already half in love. Outside after, bus stop.

NARRATOR V.O.
Dad left. Ma never moved on. It destroyed her. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Narrator asleep. Cut into dream.

(SOUND: soft bed creak into forest birds)

EXT. DECIDUOUS FOREST / OPEN FIELD - DAY (DREAM)

Moss, trillium, bleeding hearts. Narrator in white ballooned sleeves and red embroidered tunic rides a dappled mare behind Sionna—long brown hair, rose-and-warm-grass scent. They dismount in high grass. First kiss. Her eyes early-morning blue.

(SOUND: hoof soft on moss, then wind in grass)

NARRATOR
(in dream, whisper)
Hold—

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - BEDROOM - NIGHT

He bolts upright, sweating in the cold room, hand on chest.

(SOUND: his gasp, heartbeat thump in the quiet)

NARRATOR
What the hell was that?

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Purple rectangle mats. Twenty people. Narrator in t-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face in downward dog; he tucks it into his waistband. Sionna beside him, easy. Sweat, shake, then corpse pose—stillness.

(SOUND: instructor's calm count, collective breath, soft music drone)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
You caught on very quickly. Your practice is your practice.

NARRATOR
Yeah. I did like it. I just felt so dumb.

Later at the pizza parlor he works smiling, unbothered by rude customers.

(SOUND: pizza oven roar under his calm)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Nick stomps, face red, fists clenching. Ma sits blank in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam echo, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I'm sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? With who?

NARRATOR
I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That's all.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. Don't you think Ma deserves better? Look at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out. Narrator rubs his temple.

NARRATOR V.O.
One time in high school he sprayed lighter fluid on a kid's yard and set it on fire. Everybody's afraid of Nick.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - DAY

Narrator at a table with a yoga book and a Buddhism paperback. Reads, underlining.

(SOUND: chair scrape, distant copier)

NARRATOR V.O.
Attachment makes us miserable. Happiness isn't outside. Life is suffering. Meditate on the present. Hope through changing our minds.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Phone to his ear. Ma on a game show, confused look when he sits near.

(SOUND: TV buzzer, phone line)

NARRATOR
I wish you'd come over. Meet Ma. And I read about Buddhism. I want to try meditating.

SIONNA
(on phone, filtered)
You've already tried it silly. Savasana. Corpse pose.

Ma clutches her chest, dizzy.

MA
It feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON, serious, gray at the temples, faces Narrator while Ma rests in another room.

(SOUND: wall clock tick, hallway pager)

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of a stroke.
Take her directly to the emergency room. IV medicine until stabilized.

NARRATOR
Whoa man. Are you sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
Confused. Chest pressure this afternoon. Yes.

EXT. HOSPITAL ENTRANCE - DAY

Nick's Trans Am screeches up. Nick furious. They load Ma. She holds her chest.

(SOUND: idling V8, automatic doors whoosh)

MA
Someone must be talking about me. Ringing in my ears.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma dozes. Narrator asleep in the chair after Sionna's lunch visit. Dream crashes in.

(SOUND: monitor beep slows into battle clang)

EXT. MEDIEVAL BATTLEFIELD - DAY (DREAM)

Chaos, blood, steel. Narrator over a dying man.

DYING MAN
Save her.

Nick runs at him through falling bodies—eyes fire—and drives a huge knife into Narrator's chest.

NICK
That you are, little brother.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Narrator gasps awake, hand on sternum, betrayed nausea on his face. Ma still sleeping.

(SOUND: his ragged breath, monitor steady beep)

INT. DRUGSTORE - DAY

Sionna and Narrator at the counter with a blue plastic weekly pill organizer, days labeled, morning/night.

(SOUND: pharmacy intercom, bag rustle)

SIONNA
This way nobody gets confused.

NARRATOR
First time in my life things are starting to go right.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Ma tucked in earlier. Narrator shows Nick the organizer. Nick boots on the footstool, beet-red, smirking.

(SOUND: TV cop-show siren, boot thud on wood)

NICK
Oh, holy shit. Aren't you nurse Nancy. How'd you come up with this?

NARRATOR
Sionna helped. She's really smart.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger. If you must know.

NARRATOR
Stop it Nick! You haven't even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard! You're so goddamn hateful. Was Ma on crack when she was pregnant with you?

WHACK. Nick's open hand slaps Narrator's head. Ear rings. Nick storms out, door slam.

(SOUND: slap crack, door crash, ear-ring tone)

NARRATOR V.O.
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Lilacs. End of year. YOUNG NARRATOR and BOBBY SWENSON doubled over laughing. MRS. STARK bears down, glasses on a beaded chain. YOUNG NICK intercepts, inch taller than her.

(SOUND: recess roar, then hush)

YOUNG NICK
He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him. He'll never act up again. I guarantee it.

MRS. STARK
YOU need to follow your brother's example.

Later, bedroom: Young Nick musses his brother's hair.

YOUNG NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT (MONTHS LATER)

Ma and Sionna chat softly; Ma lights up. Nick rare, suede jacket, boots. Narrator stands firmer—calmer eyes from meditation practice.

(SOUND: TV low, keys tossed in basket)

NARRATOR
I'm going to community college. Bachelor's in business. Student loan. Manage a restaurant maybe.

NICK
How the hell will you pay? How you going to watch Ma? This chick telling you what to do?

NARRATOR
This was my idea. It would mean a lot if you could be on my side for once.

Nick leans in, voice low so Ma can't hear, grinning about old threats.

NICK
Donny Amstel. I told him our family had mafia connections. He believed me.
Nothing is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, not this girl.
Bros before ho's. You get me?

Narrator forces a thin smile, jaw tight.

NARRATOR
Sure Nick. I get you.

EXT. CITY SIDEWALK - DAY

Narrator and Sionna walk. Skateboard kids laugh at them. He bristles; she doesn't.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels, distant traffic)

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we deal with the world.
Loving kindness. Open our hearts even to kids laughing at us.

He kisses her—slow.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Smoke cloud. NICK, BOB, SAMMY with a pipe and Buds. Narrator on a rocker, enduring it.

(SOUND: classic rock muffled, lighter flint, wet cough-laughs)

SAMMY
You being pussy whipped? Need a get out of jail free card?

NARRATOR
You three wouldn't recognize class if it bit your ass.

NICK
He's got the perfect girlfriend. Wrapped right now.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Packed Saturday. AC/DC under the noise. Nick spots LINDSEY—blonde, pink sweater—with a dark-haired man, arm around her. Nick's face goes crazy-calm.

(SOUND: AC/DC "Have a Drink on Me," pool crack, sudden room hush)

NICK
What's up Lindsey. Who's she with? Who's that asshole?

LINDSEY
Oh, just an old friend.

DARK-HAIRED MAN
You got a problem with me? You're in my face like a little bitch.

Nick's fists clench. Bob and Sammy ready. Narrator steps between.

NARRATOR
Nick, leave it. You don't even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're next if you don't get out of my face.

Narrator stays in his face.

NARRATOR
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego Nick!

Nick's right fist draws back in slow motion toward Narrator's face. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Head-butt, scramble. Lindsey pulls her man toward the door.

(SOUND: fist leather creak, shout pile-up, glass rattle)

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS - NIGHT

Narrator runs full out—sidewalks, obstacles, cold air knifing lungs—until he hits Sionna's door.

(SOUND: shoes pounding pavement, his heaving breath, heartbeat)

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. He pours the story. She holds his face.

(SOUND: his residual pant, apartment hush, clock tick)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that.
He is a controlling ass. You are becoming bravely compassionate.

NARRATOR
My purpose—what is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it.

She draws him down, head in her lap, massages his scalp. They drift.

EXT. LAKE SHORE - DAY (DREAM)

Black-and-white world goes color. Sionna rises from water in white, sleeves like wings. Giant Nick with a whirling mace shrinks under her invisible force and vanishes. Embrace.

(SOUND: wind symphony, water lap, mace chains that fall silent)

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

They jolt awake tangled on the couch. Kitchen clock: 3 a.m.

(SOUND: clock tick loud in dark)

NARRATOR
You were an angel. You protected us from Nick.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right. Bed.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months of routine compressed: pill box filled; Sionna cooks butternut casserole; Ma listens to reading then forgets content but not the kindness. Nick absent. Narrator covers with lies about poker and gym.

(SOUND: casserole dish set down, soft page turns, game-show theme)

NARRATOR V.O.
I was writing on Descartes. I think, therefore I am. Something had to give.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Ancient stone, lion-head carvings, plaque 1898. NARRATOR at a chest-high counter, then with OFFICER DUPUIS—big, graying, thin mustache. Styrofoam cup shakes in Narrator's hands.

(SOUND: wall of voices, phones, radio squelch)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Nicholas Olson and Olivia Olson.
Hit broadside at Werner and Lowe about eight p.m.
Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

Narrator stares through him. Water. Phone. Call to Sionna's voicemail.

(SOUND: his breath breaking, distant siren through stone)

INT. HOSPITAL ICU HALLWAY - DAY

Through glass: Nick's wild fire-colored hair, white bandages, wires, chest tube, leg huge under sheet. Narrator looks once, turns away from the nurse.

(SOUND: multi-monitor symphony, shoe squeaks on waxed floor)

NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Empty chair. Empty afghan. Narrator crumples, three-year-old bawling, pulling hair.

(SOUND: raw sobbing, fists on floor)

NARRATOR
Die you fucking jackass. Die. Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

Sionna enters, holds him, rocks.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out.

INT. SACRED HEART FUNERAL HOME - DAY

Moderately priced maple casket. Sionna steady beside him. Father Joseph arrangements. Small funeral later: few neighbors, old coworkers, Bob solemn. Graveside at St. Mary's. Narrator throws a handful of dirt. Muted thunk on the lid.

(SOUND: thunk of dirt on wood, soft rosary murmur, wind in cemetery trees)

NARRATOR V.O.
I realized I would be the only son there. Rightly so, since he killed her.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Five days later. Nick sallow, black-green bruises, oxygen tube, chest tube, IV. He flicks the TV off.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, remote click, hallway cart)

NICK
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears cut his bruised face. Narrator stands dead-eyed.

NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice. She's at peace. Top of the hill at St. Mary's.

NICK
Three Jack and Cokes before I remembered church. Cops saying vehicular manslaughter.
I was almost killed too. Why would I want to hurt Ma?

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick flares; Narrator doesn't flinch—Nick is trapped in tubes.

NARRATOR
Think about it.

He turns and walks out.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

Late fall sun on the marker. Narrator sets golden mums in the metal vase from his water bottle.

(SOUND: wind in dry leaves, far highway)

NARRATOR V.O.
Mother. I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Narrator now head pie man. Tommy fumbles the standing Friday order. Tony tosses car keys; Narrator delivers Mike Jones's half-pepperoni half-mushroom-olive in record time, returns cold-flushed. Tony grips his shoulder.

(SOUND: oven roar, door chime, keys jingle)

TONY
Man, that was quick. How would you like to do more deliveries?
I'm sorry for your ma. You look tired. I want to help.

NARRATOR V.O.
I had no father, no mother, no brother. Sometimes you just need a parent.

INT. SIONNA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Narrator startles awake beside her.

(SOUND: his jolt, sheets, her sleepy murmur)

NARRATOR
Dream. Your parents' farm. You had a baby. Instead of a face— a small pizza.

SIONNA
(laughing soft)
You work too much. Also you're giving birth to a new idea.

NARRATOR V.O.
What if we guarantee half-hour delivery or it's free. Like Domino's. Talk to Tony tomorrow.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Bob enters, pupils wide, animated. Narrator's face hardens.

(SOUND: street bell on door, kitchen clatter)

BOB
Nick barely survived. Cops hounding him. Might lock him up. He's your brother, man.

NARRATOR
He accidentally killed one person. I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma dead.
I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs up, hands raised.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY (NICK'S WORLD)

Nick—thinner, limping, gray-edged—paces, smokes under a no-smoking life. Court papers. Jack craving.

(SOUND: lighter, floor pace, traffic through thin glass)

NICK
(to himself)
That little bitch. All her fault. School and crazy ideas. He had one job. Take care of Ma.

INT. BUS - DAY

Nick on route 10 among bag ladies and kids. Jaw set. Climbs off into gray freeze. Walks to a duplex with dead summer pots.

(SOUND: bus pneumatic doors, cold wind, his boots on grit)

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Sionna opens a crack, then wider. Gray cat slinks. She offers coffee. Nick refuses, crowds her, spit-anger.

(SOUND: door chain, cat soft mew, his rising voice)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail.
He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive. You confused his stupid brain.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally. What makes you think I'm responsible?

He walks her into the hall wall. Hand on her throat. Switchblade flicks open—Swiss Army. Edge to the soft place between collarbone and breast.

(SOUND: blade snick, her choked squeak, fridge hum far off)

NICK
Break it off tonight. Cash in a week. Leak one word—I have friends.
If I wanted to kill you I could kill you right now.

Front door WHAM. Narrator fills the frame.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Narrator grabs the knife arm. Elbow to Narrator's head. Blade drives into Sionna. Red blooms. She sinks. Nick wipes the blade on her shirt, bolts.

(SOUND: body thud, wet gasp, running boots, door bang)

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - HALLWAY - DAY

Narrator on his knees, rips flannel, stacks hands on the wound near her left shoulder, blood soaking camisole. Pale face. Shallow breath.

(SOUND: his panicked breath, blood drip on wood, rising sirens)

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay.

Paramedics flood in—monitors, needle, gurney. A COP pulls blood-soaked Narrator aside.

COP
You say your brother stabbed your girlfriend?

NARRATOR
Sionna. Sionna McAllister. Nick Olson. He ran.

INT. HOSPITAL WAITING AREA - NIGHT

Hours. Then: she lives—punctured lung, shoulder repair, foam pillow, narcotics. Later months: blank zombie stare in a rocking chair at home. Narrator kneels before her.

(SOUND: waiting-room TV murmur into home clock tick)

NARRATOR
You know I love you. You're safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt I can't go on with you.
Maybe we just need a break. Therapist. Mom and Dad's for a while.

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves for work early, free-falling.

NARRATOR V.O.
She was my life guide. My fingers slipped off the cliff. I had no one now.

EXT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN STREET - DAY (YEARS LATER)

Gray blanket sky. Mist. Hooded walkers. NARRATOR, mid-thirties now, fuller shoulders, cleaner coat, walks past a keyboard busker. Signage: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA under a medical building.

(SOUND: light rain on hoods, ferry horn distant, keyboard disco riff)

NARRATOR V.O.
Many years. Thanks to Tony I built a chain. GPS now, not paper maps. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

Second floor, Puget Sound view, cruise ship, ferry. Cinnamon roll like a halo. He can't eat. Flash memory: blood hallway, pressure, Sionna leaving.

(SOUND: ferry horn, plate set down, market crowd below)

NARRATOR V.O.
Outer flesh heals. Inner wounds linger. She blamed me in the end. Not the enlightened being I thought.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Theme: gratefulness. Narrator on a mat among ten others. Eyes closed.

(SOUND: soft chant, collective exhale)

NARRATOR V.O.
Shakespeare—things are neither good nor bad but thinking makes it so. I still feel the void. And sorrow for him.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Warm. Orange-and-white cat BOZO on the couch. Narrator reads, drifts into dream.

(SOUND: rain on glass, cat purr)

EXT. GREEN HILLS ROAD - DAY (DREAM)

Hyper-colored grass. An Indian SAGE in white wrap walks beside him. A dirty pizza box on the path. Narrator judges it. Sage stops him.

(SOUND: wind clean, then stern voice)

SAGE
Why haven't you learned anything? Stop judging. The pizza box is here only when we view it.
All things are illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

Box and sage gone. Joy hits like light.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Narrator wakes smiling, grabs a notebook, writes fast. Bozo against his stomach.

(SOUND: pen scratch, rain soft)

NARRATOR
I have to see Nick. I have to put this behind me.

Laptop open—flight to Milwaukee via Denver booked.

EXT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY

CHRIS, blonde messy bun, hoop earrings, stained apron. He finally asks her name; they set a play date. Sun breaks the drizzle.

(SOUND: milk steamer, street laugh, cup set down)

INT. RENTAL CAR / EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Milwaukee again. Same brick fortress, dumpsters instead of dented cans. Hispanic couple with stroller. His face hollow-chocolate sad, then set.

(SOUND: rental engine, stroller wheels, his door close)

EXT. OLD PIZZA PARLOR BLOCK - DAY

Now CENTRAL COSMETOLOGY—pink scissors sign. Dark inside. He almost smiles at the loss.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant traffic)

INT. DELI - DAY

Reuben and fries. BOB—grayer, thicker, carved lines—grease on his mouth by the window. Double-take. Bear hug. Patrons stare.

(SOUND: deli buzz, his cackle, plate clink)

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro? You look so grown up. You came back.
You visiting Nick? I couldn't do it no more. He looks so sad and guilty.

NARRATOR
I need to talk things out. Let the shit go. I own a pizza chain in Seattle.

BOB
You got out. I'm proud of you, man.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD DUPLEX - DAY

Smaller, grayer paint, naked porch, weeds in gravel. He parks, stares, does not knock.

(SOUND: kids on bikes, shepherd dog bark, his engine idle)

NARRATOR V.O.
I loved her so much. I just want her to be happy. Leave her alone. Move on.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

Oppressive brick. Gravel lot. Metal detectors. Narrator waits, coffee, pulse up. A flabby OFFICER briefs the glass rules.

(SOUND: keys jangle, steel doors lock-unlock, fluorescent hum)

INT. PRISON VISITING BOOTH - DAY

Thick plexiglass. Phones. NICK enters opposite—scrawny versus former bulk, gray at sides, thin on top, forehead lined, watery bagged eyes, prison scrubs. They stare.

(SOUND: muffled hall roar, phone plastic pickup click)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Routine down. Parole hearing maybe two years.

NARRATOR
That's good for you.

Narrator leans in; fifteen minutes on the clock.

NARRATOR
I need you to listen. Even though you're my brother, I'm no longer interested in seeing you.
You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down my whole life.

Nick's eyes shine. Narrator does not stop—voice steady.

NARRATOR
You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.
Now the important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. It ends your control forever.

Tears spill down Nick's cheeks onto the gray shelf.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna.
You're more than I ever was. I deserve to live in here.

GUARD
(O.S.)
Time's almost up.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. Expanding. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you.

Guard ends it. Nick smiles, mouthed bye, shuffled out. Narrator sits with the yellow receiver, light in his chest.

(SOUND: phone settled, door buzz, his long exhale)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT TERMINAL - DAY

Rolling green hills past the rental window. Then airport throng. Narrator floats through security lines, nuts and tea in hand, boarding queue.

(SOUND: tire hum on asphalt, then terminal murmur, boarding scanner beep)

NARRATOR V.O.
Somewhere she's out there. Happy. I have to believe that. Weight of thirty years—gone.

He looks at the boarding pass in his hand. Nick's proud voice echoes in memory.

NICK
(V.O.)
President Olson.

NARRATOR
(whisper, to himself)
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He walks down the jet bridge into light.

(SOUND: jetway rumble, then engines spooling toward quiet)

NARRATOR V.O.
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END